    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1871, Deatsville opened its first post office and the South and North Alabama Railroad arrived, later becoming part of the Louisville & Nashville Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/deatsville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Between 1900 and the Great Depression, Deatsville boomed, boasting four general stores, two cotton gins, two boarding houses, two sawmills, and a grist mill.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/deatsville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the late 1850s, Deatsville already had a sash, door, and blind factory, plus a grist mill and sawmill, showing early industrial activity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/deatsville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Portions of Deatsville sit at the edge of Jordan Lake, giving residents a waterfront view.",
